atmosphere-debug only uses `createAtmosphereDome` (not the WorldManager's dome), so as long as we keep the return type of `createAtmosphereDome` as `AtmosphereDome` and its material as a ShaderMaterial, we're fine. The cleanest approach: keep the `AtmosphereDome` interface (with ShaderMaterial), and add a separate interface `FlatHaze` with the same operational shape (mesh, set, dispose, haze), and in WorldManager type the field as a union: `AtmosphereDome | FlatHaze`. However, WorldManager.setAtmosphereView calls `this.dome.set(...)` — the union is fine as long as both have set. `this.dome.mesh` is also fine.

Additions to atmosphere-dome.ts:

```ts
export interface FlatHaze {
  mesh: THREE.Mesh;
  material: THREE.MeshBasicMaterial;
  readonly haze: number;
  set(haze: number, atmoColor: THREE.Color, skyColor?: THREE.Color): void;
  dispose(): void;
}

export type AtmosphereLayer = AtmosphereDome | FlatHaze;

export function createFlatHaze(radiusU: number): FlatHaze { ... }
```

Type of the WorldManager.dome field: currently `private readonly dome: AtmosphereDome;` → `private dome: AtmosphereLayer;`.

Check the usage sites of dome.material in WorldManager: only around line 258 in the header comment. setAtmosphereView uses dome.set + mesh.position. dispose calls dome.dispose(). Good.

